+			<p>They laughed and chatted all the way home; and little <span epub:type="z3998:personal-name">Baptiste</span>, up behind, thought that monsieur and mademoiselle were in charming spirits. But both felt ill at ease; the friendly frankness was disturbed, the sunshine had a shadow over it, and despite their apparent gayety, there was a secret discontent in the heart of each.</p>
 			<p>“Shall we see you this evening, <i xml:lang="fr">mon frère</i>?” asked Amy as they parted at her aunt’s door.</p>
 			<p>“Unfortunately I have an engagement. <i xml:lang="fr">Au revoir, mademoiselle</i>,” and Laurie bent as if to kiss her hand, in the foreign fashion, which became him better than many men. Something in his face made Amy say quickly and warmly⁠—</p>
 			<p>“No; be yourself with me, Laurie, and part in the good old way. I’d rather have a hearty English handshake than all the sentimental salutations in France.”</p>
